1a:["$","p","2",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Run it in Claude Code, Cursor or Codex, opened in your project, so it can read the code and make the changes. A website won't use the Android ones and an app won't use speculation rules, which is why it skips whatever doesn't fit."}]
1b:["$","p","3",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Below is what each of the twenty actually is, so you know what Claude just built."}]
1c:["$","h2","4",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"1. Skeleton loaders (Apple)"}]
1d:["$","p","5",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Gray blocks in the shape of the real layout while it loads, instead of a spinner in the middle of a blank screen. The app feels faster because the page is already there, and nothing jumps when the content lands. Only show it if loading takes longer than about 300ms, or it flashes."}]
1e:["$","h2","6",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"2. Optimistic updates (React)"}]
1f:["$","p","7",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"You tap like and the heart fills in right away, while the request goes out in the background. If it fails, the heart goes back and you get told. React 19 has a hook for this called useOptimistic. Use it for small things that almost always work, never for payments."}]
20:["$","h2","8",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"3. A command menu (Linear)"}]
21:["$","p","9",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Cmd+K opens a search box with every page and action in the app, so nobody has to remember which menu something lives in. Linear made it the standard. Worth it in any app people open every day."}]
22:["$","h2","10",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"4. Shortcut hints (Superhuman)"}]
23:["$","p","11",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"When you do something with the mouse, Superhuman shows you the keyboard shortcut for it. People learn the shortcuts just by using the app."}]
24:["$","h2","12",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"5. Undo toasts (Gmail)"}]
25:["$","p","13",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Gmail archives the email the second you click and gives you Undo, instead of asking if you're sure first. Confirm dialogs get clicked through without reading anyway. Undo Send works the same way, it just holds the email for a few seconds before sending."}]
26:["$","h2","14",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"6. Stale-while-revalidate (Vercel)"}]
27:["$","p","15",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Show what you already have cached instantly, fetch the fresh version in the background, and swap it in when it arrives. Vercel's SWR library is named after it. Going back to a screen you just left should never show a spinner."}]
28:["$","h2","16",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"7. Prerendering (Chrome)"}]
29:["$","p","17",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Chrome can load the next page while your mouse is still hovering the link, so by the time you click it's already there. It's one script tag called speculation rules, and browsers that don't support it ignore it."}]
2a:["$","h2","18",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"8. BlurHash placeholders (Wolt)"}]
2b:["$","p","19",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Wolt made BlurHash, which turns a photo into a string of about 20 to 30 characters that decodes into a blurry version of it. You send that string with the rest of your data and show the blur while the real image loads, so the screen has the right colors from the first frame."}]
2c:["$","h2","20",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"9. View transitions (Chrome)"}]
2d:["$","p","21",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"The browser animates between two pages for you, like a thumbnail growing into the full photo when you tap it. It used to take a whole animation library. Now it's a few lines of CSS, and browsers without it just switch instantly."}]
2e:["$","h2","22",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"10. Swipe-to-dismiss sheets (Apple)"}]
2f:["$","p","23",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"The sheet that slides up from the bottom on iOS, and you drag it back down to close it. It follows your finger, and a quick flick closes it even if you only moved it a little. Vaul does this on the web."}]
30:["$","h2","24",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"11. Interruptible animations (Apple)"}]
31:["$","p","25",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"You can grab anything while it's still moving and it turns around from where it is, instead of finishing the animation before it listens to you. Apple's 2018 talk on fluid interfaces is where this comes from. Springs do it, CSS keyframe animations don't."}]
32:["$","h2","26",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"12. Predictive back (Android)"}]
33:["$","p","27",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Start the back swipe on Android and you see a preview of the screen you're going back to, and if you change your mind you just let go. Apps targeting Android 16 get it by default, so if your app overrides the back button the old way, that's the code to move."}]
34:["$","h2","28",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"13. Hit slop (React Native)"}]
35:["$","p","29",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"The touch area of a button is bigger than the button you see. A 20 pixel icon still gets a 44 point target, which is Apple's minimum. React Native calls the prop hitSlop, and on the web it's just padding."}]
36:["$","h2","30",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"14. Safe-area insets (Apple)"}]
37:["$","p","31",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"The notch, the Dynamic Island and the home bar cover parts of the screen. Safe-area insets tell you how much room to leave, so your bottom tab bar doesn't sit under the home indicator and your header doesn't sit under the island."}]
38:["$","h2","32",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"15. One-time-code autofill (Apple)"}]
39:["$","p","33",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"The login code from your text shows up right above the keyboard and fills in with one tap. On a website it's a single attribute on the input, autocomplete=\"one-time-code\". On iOS it's textContentType set to oneTimeCode."}]
3a:["$","h2","34",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"16. Tabular numbers (CSS)"}]
3b:["$","p","35",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"In most fonts a 1 is narrower than an 8, so a timer or a price that changes wiggles side to side. One line of CSS, font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums, makes every digit the same width and the wiggle stops."}]
3c:["$","h2","36",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"17. Balanced headlines (CSS)"}]
3d:["$","p","37",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"text-wrap: balance evens out the lines of a headline so you don't get one lonely word on the last line. text-wrap: pretty does the same for paragraphs."}]
3e:["$","h2","38",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"18. Continuous corners (Apple)"}]
3f:["$","p","39",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Apple's rounded corners use a smooth curve called a squircle instead of a quarter circle stuck onto a straight edge, which is why iOS icons look softer than everyone else's. It's one setting in SwiftUI, and Chrome can do it on the web now with corner-shape: squircle."}]
40:["$","h2","40",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"19. Idempotency keys (Stripe)"}]
41:["$","p","41",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Someone taps Pay on bad wifi, nothing happens, so they tap it again. With an idempotency key, the retry carries the same ID as the first try, and Stripe returns the first result instead of charging them twice. Use the same idea on your own API for anything that creates something."}]
42:["$","h2","42",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"20. Safe triangle (Amazon)"}]
43:["$","p","43",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"On Amazon's old mega menu, you could move your mouse diagonally toward a submenu across the other rows and it wouldn't switch on you. The menu worked out which way you were heading and waited. Floating UI calls it safePolygon, and plenty of menu libraries already have it built in."}]
44:["$","h2","44",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"Where to go next"}]
45:["$","ul","45",{"className":"mt-5 divide-y divide-line border-y border-line","children":[["$","li","/vault/10-signs-website-ai-slop-design-rules",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"/vault/10-signs-website-ai-slop-design-rules","className":"group flex items-start gap-4 py-3.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"min-w-0 flex-1","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"block font-semibold text-ink transition-colors group-hover:text-accent","children":"10 signs your website is AI slop"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 block text-[15px] leading-[1.6] text-ink-soft","children":"The design rules. These details go on top of a page that doesn't look like a template."}]]}],["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 text-ink-dim transition-colors group-hover:text-accent","children":"→"}]]}]}],["$","li","/vault/20-things-before-you-launch",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"/vault/20-things-before-you-launch","className":"group flex items-start gap-4 py-3.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"min-w-0 flex-1","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"block font-semibold text-ink transition-colors group-hover:text-accent","children":"20 things before you launch"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 block text-[15px] leading-[1.6] text-ink-soft","children":"The housekeeping list for the week before you ship."}]]}],["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 text-ink-dim transition-colors group-hover:text-accent","children":"→"}]]}]}]]}]
46:["$","section",null,{"className":"read-more","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"split-title","children":"Keep reading"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"posts mt-8","children":[["$","$L8","omega-agent-orchestration",{"href":"/vault/omega-agent-orchestration","className":"post","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"cover","children":[["$","img",null,{"src":"/covers/omega-agent-orchestration.webp","width":1200,"height":800,"alt":"","loading":"lazy","decoding":"async"}],["$","canvas",null,{"aria-hidden":"true"}]]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"post-meta","children":[["$","b",null,{"children":"Building"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"6 min"}]]}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"post-title","children":"The Omega setup: Opus plans, Haiku agents do the work"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"post-excerpt line-clamp-3","children":"The Claude Code setup from the video. Opus 5.5 on extra effort runs the job, Haiku 5.5 agents do the work for a fortieth of the price, every agent gets a goal and a check, memory gets compacted early, and the skills improve after every session. One prompt that sets it all up, then what each part does."}]]}],["$","$L8","5-videos-ai-engineer",{"href":"/vault/5-videos-ai-engineer","className":"post","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"cover","children":[["$","img",null,{"src":"/covers/5-videos-ai-engineer.webp","width":1200,"height":800,"alt":"","loading":"lazy","decoding":"async"}],["$","canvas",null,{"aria-hidden":"true"}]]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"post-meta","children":[["$","b",null,{"children":"Building"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"5 min"}]]}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"post-title","children":"5 videos that turn vibe coding into vibe engineering"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"post-excerpt line-clamp-3","children":"Everyone knows how to vibe code. These five talks are how you engineer with AI instead. All five links up top, then what to change in how you work with Claude Code, Codex or Cursor after each one."}]]}],["$","$L8","folder-method-one-ai-agent",{"href":"/vault/folder-method-one-ai-agent","className":"post","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"cover","children":[["$","img",null,{"src":"/covers/folder-method-one-ai-agent.webp","width":1200,"height":800,"alt":"","loading":"lazy","decoding":"async"}],["$","canvas",null,{"aria-hidden":"true"}]]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"post-meta","children":[["$","b",null,{"children":"Building"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"7 min"}]]}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"post-title","children":"The folder method: one AI agent that survives every model update"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"post-excerpt line-clamp-3","children":"The full guide from the video. Skip the 300 agents. Use the one you already have, give it a map and one folder per job, and write the proof before the playbook, so a new model costs you a few lines instead of a week. Starter folder and all five prompts included."}]]}]]}]]}]
